    ("Assistant & AI", "assistant.html"), ("Proven in delivery", "proven.html"))


# ---------------- the outbound check (deep-dive, no screenshots by design) ----
gate = page_head(
    "Before you send — Engineering Workspace",
    "The outbound check: retrieves the clauses your draft relies on, reads the whole section, and finds what would get it rejected before it leaves.")
gate += """
<header class="phero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="crumb"><a href="index.html">Engineering Workspace</a> / Before you send</div>
  <div class="eyebrow">In depth</div>
  <h1>Before you send</h1>
  <p class="sub" style="margin-top:16px">Document review normally reads what ARRIVES. This reads what
  is about to LEAVE. The asymmetry is the whole point: by the time a request has gone out, the cost
  of the mistake inside it is already fixed.</p>
</div></header>
<div class="wrap">

  __GATE_FILM__

  <div class="block"><div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size:24px">Two speeds, deliberately</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">The instant pass runs on the wording alone. It costs
      nothing, returns in a second, and is safe to run while somebody is still typing. The full check
      spends real effort: it goes and retrieves the specification sections the draft leans on and
      reads them whole, which is the only way to catch a contradiction nobody remembered.</p>
      <div class="checks">
        <div>Instant: editions, absolute claims, undeclared compliance, self-contradicting limits, missing response dates</div>
        <div>Full: the governing sections retrieved and read end to end, with the contradicting clause quoted</div>
        <div>Half an answer always beats a spinner — instant findings survive if the full pass fails</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="caps">
        <div class="cap"><b>One overstated compliance claim</b><span>and the reviewer reads everything else you wrote with suspicion.</span></div>
        <div class="cap"><b>One deviation you did not declare</b><span>and it comes back, and it is remembered next time.</span></div>
        <div class="cap"><b>One edition the contract does not bind</b><span>and the rejection is a technicality that still costs a full cycle.</span></div>
        <div class="cap"><b>No required response date</b><span>and the answer arrives whenever it happens to suit.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div></div>

  <div class="block"><div class="split rev">
    <div>
      <div class="caps">
        <div class="cap"><b>Editions</b><span>Which edition of each code the contract binds, which edition is actually on the shelf, and where the two disagree.</span></div>
        <div class="cap"><b>Conflicts</b><span>Two requirements about the same component that cannot both be satisfied — a US listing here, a British standard there.</span></div>
        <div class="cap"><b>Acceptable makers</b><span>Every clause that already names manufacturers, with what their products must satisfy. Arguments you already own.</span></div>
        <div class="cap"><b>Authority file</b><span>What the contract requires of the code authority, what has been issued for its review, and what is missing.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size:24px">The specification, read against itself</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">These run once per specification revision rather than
      once per question, and they are the reason the check can be specific. A conflict found at
      tender is a priced qualification. The same conflict found at procurement is a delay and an
      argument. And a clause that already names your manufacturer as acceptable is the strongest
      sentence your letter can carry — most of them are never used, because nobody knows the clause
      is there.</p>
      <div class="checks">
        <div>Deterministic where it can be: an answer always traces to the sentence it came from</div>
        <div>Runs on your own server — no per-question cost, nothing leaves</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div></div>

  <div class="block"><div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size:24px">What the change obliges elsewhere</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">Substitutions are never local. Change one fitting's
      thread and you have quietly obliged new couplings, nozzles, reducers, caps and the fire
      authority's inlets — none of which appears in the request that started it. Mixed threads in a
      tower is a hazard discovered at commissioning, which is the worst possible moment to discover
      anything. The workspace walks the dependency and lists what else the change touches: other
      components, approved submittals, procured items, drawings, other trades.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size:24px">And what the Engineer already said</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">A project carries years of clarifications, each with a
      written response from the party who will answer the next one. That is institutional memory
      sitting as files, findable only by whoever was there at the time. The workspace turns it into
      an answer: this has been ruled on, here is the ruling, here is how it was reasoned — and it
      predicts the likely reply from what this Engineer actually wrote, not from a generic model of
      consultants.</p>
      <div class="checks">
        <div>A response from your own side is labelled as such, never passed off as authority</div>
        <div>Survives staff turnover — the value compounds the longer a project runs</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div></div>

  <div class="block">
    <div class="capshead"><div class="eyebrow">The other half</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">And it proves you were right.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="font-size:14px">Everything above stops a mistake leaving. The record is
    what protects you when the answer is slow.</p></div>
    <div class="caps">
      <div class="cap"><b>The response record</b><span>How long this project actually takes to answer, measured from clarifications carrying both dates — median, ninetieth percentile and worst. A number measured from the project beats a period quoted from the conditions.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>The evidence chain</b><span>For any one reference: issued, allowed, answered or not, and what it consumed. The exhibit itself, not a paragraph claiming one exists.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Notices on record</b><span>Which entitlements were noticed and when — the part people forget until it matters.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>The handover dossier</b><span>Closeout obligations counted from the specification clauses themselves and set against what is actually filed, long before the month the building is due.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Bilingual output</b><span>The same document in Arabic and English block for block, with clause numbers, standard references and quoted contract text left in English — what a regional reviewer actually wants.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Cited history</b><span>Every answer the workspace gave, with the pages it came from. Your defensible trail.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <div class="capshead"><div class="eyebrow">And beyond the paper</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">The building, and the company.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="font-size:14px">Two places the same evidence goes further: out to the
    site, and up to the head office.</p></div>
    <div class="caps">
      <div class="cap"><b>Is this what was approved?</b><span>Photograph an installed nameplate and it is read against the approved submittal for that component — the manufacturer, the model, the listings. Not a checklist asking whether it looks neat.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Honest about a photograph</b><span>It reports that a listing mark is not visible in the image. It never claims an item is unlisted, and an unreadable photo produces one finding about the photo.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Say it once</b><span>Speak a snag while walking and it becomes the right record — defect to the issues register, question to an RFI — with the references you said and the sheet you had open. The listening runs on the phone, so it costs nothing.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Across every job</b><span>Which consultant actually answers, which return reason repeats on four projects, and which specification conflict is already sitting in the next tender document.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="block" id="break">
    <div class="eyebrow">The test we invite</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">Do not trust the demo. Try to break it.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">A polished demo proves nothing. The honest test of a
    document engine is a trick question, asked on a real specification. Bring a sanitized project
    to your trial and put these three traps in front of it:</p>
    <div class="vals" style="margin-top:26px">
      <div class="val reveal"><div class="num">01</div><h3>The conflicting clause</h3>
        <p>Ask about a requirement where two clauses of the same specification disagree. A keyword tool returns the sentence that agrees with you. The check is built to read the section whole and surface the one that does not.</p></div>
      <div class="val reveal"><div class="num">02</div><h3>The wrong edition</h3>
        <p>Cite a section number from a code edition your contract does not bind. The editions register exists exactly for this: the rejection that looks like a technicality, caught before it costs a full cycle.</p></div>
      <div class="val reveal"><div class="num">03</div><h3>The ruling that changed everything</h3>
        <p>Ask a question whose answer a previous Engineer response quietly reversed. The precedent search answers from your own correspondence, with the letter and the date.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink3);margin-top:18px">If it misses one, tell us exactly
    what it missed. That report is worth more to us than a compliment, and it is how the engine
    got this far.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="ctaband reveal">
    <div style="flex:1;min-width:260px"><h3>Hand it one of your own requests.</h3>
    <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2);margin-top:4px">That is the whole demonstration. A real account inside a realistic demo project, in one minute.</div></div>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="index.html#try">Try the live app &#8594;</a>
  </div>
  <div class="pn"><a href="index.html">&#8592; Back to the overview</a><a href="dashboard.html">Dashboard &#8594;</a></div>
</div>"""
gate = gate.replace("__GATE_FILM__",
                    ytone("gate", "The outbound-check film: a draft read against its own sources."))
write_page("gate.html", gate + FOOT, priority="0.9", changefreq="weekly")


# ---------------- proof page ----------------
proven = page_head(
    "Proven on a live mega-project — Engineering Workspace",
    "Not a prototype: built inside the delivery of a live mega-project and used every working day by the team delivering it.")
proven += f"""
<header class="phero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="crumb"><a href="index.html">Engineering Workspace</a> / Proven in delivery</div>
  <div class="eyebrow">Built in delivery, not in a demo lab</div>
  <h1>It was proven before it was sold.</h1>
  <p class="sub" style="margin-top:16px">Engineering Workspace was not designed as a product and then
  looked for a project. It was written inside the delivery of a live mega-project by the engineer
  running that delivery, on the days the problems appeared — and it has carried that project's
  documents, workflows and correspondence every working day since.</p>
</div></header>
<div class="wrap">
  <div class="pnums">
    <div class="pnum reveal"><div class="v">72,000+</div><div class="l">Documents indexed</div>
      <div class="s">Contracts, specifications, drawings, submittals, letters and reports — one live project's archive, searchable to the page.</div></div>
    <div class="pnum reveal"><div class="v">5,300+</div><div class="l">Sign-offs carried</div>
      <div class="s">Deliverable rows moved through real approval chains — originate, discipline check, approve, DC check, issue.</div></div>
    <div class="pnum reveal"><div class="v">14</div><div class="l">Engineers in parallel</div>
      <div class="s">Working the same registers and the same chains at once, reconciled without a single lost revision.</div></div>
    <div class="pnum reveal"><div class="v">EN + AR</div><div class="l">Both languages, scanned</div>
      <div class="s">Stamped drawings and scanned Arabic letters read by OCR and searched alongside native text.</div></div>
  </div>

  {ytone("ownit", "The ownership film: your server, your record, your data staying home.")}

  <div class="block" id="measured">
    <div class="eyebrow">No mystery numbers</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">How each figure was measured.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">Every number on this site is a reading from one live
    workspace, not a projection. Here is exactly where each one comes from, so you can ask us to
    show you the same reading live.</p>
    <div class="caps" style="margin-top:26px">
      <div class="cap"><b>72,000+ documents indexed</b><span>The document count of the project knowledge base, read from the workspace's own index health panel. It includes specifications, drawings, correspondence and scanned letters, English and Arabic, OCRed and searchable to the page.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>5,300+ sign-offs carried</b><span>The count of deliverable revisions moved through originate, check, approve, document control and issue chains on the live engineering deliverables list, read from the workflow record.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>14 engineers in parallel</b><span>Distinct user accounts acting on the same registers and chains inside the same working weeks, read from the audit trail.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>43 calculators</b><span>Count them on the calculators page; each carries its formula and assumptions.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink3);margin-top:18px">These are first-party readings
    from a confidential project record, so the record itself is not public. On a serious
    evaluation we will happily show the readings live on a screen share, or under NDA.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="block"><div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size:24px">Every feature has a scar behind it</h2>
      <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">The follow-up engine exists because replies went missing.
      The claims ledger exists because entitlement had to be reconstructed from twelve folders at the
      worst possible moment. The scope screen exists because a consultant comment quietly added work
      that nobody registered. Nothing in this workspace was invented to fill a feature grid — each part
      was written the week its absence cost something real.</p>
      <div class="checks">
        <div>Written by a practising MEP manager, on a live delivery — not by a team who read about one</div>
        <div>Every workflow shape in the product is a shape that had to survive an actual submission</div>
        <div>The capacities quoted here are measured on that project, not extrapolated from a benchmark</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div><div class="shot reveal"><img src="img/edl.webp" alt="The deliverables list in daily use"></div>
      <div class="shotcap">The deliverable list that carried the sign-offs — day after day.</div></div>
  </div></div>

  <div class="plog">
    <div class="row"><div class="when">The archive</div><div class="what">
      <b>Ask a 72,000-document archive and get the page</b>
      <span>Indexing, OCR and retrieval tuned against a real mixed archive — native PDFs, scanned
      transmittals, stamped drawing sheets, Arabic correspondence. Answers cite the document, the
      revision and the page, so an engineer can check the source in one click rather than trust a summary.</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="when">The chains</div><div class="what">
      <b>Approval workflows that survived audit</b>
      <span>Named steps, named owners, working-day clocks and a full revision history per deliverable —
      including who acted for whom, and when. Reconstructing "what happened on P02" takes seconds,
      because the record was written as the work happened.</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="when">The correspondence</div><div class="what">
      <b>Nothing waits in silence</b>
      <span>Incoming mail tracked to the project, follow-ups chased on their own clocks, escalations
      raised before a reply becomes a delay event. The chase list is generated, not remembered.</span></div></div>
    <div class="row"><div class="when">The evidence</div><div class="what">
      <b>Claims built from the record you already keep</b>
      <span>Held-versus-allowed clocks, notice windows and excess days come out of the registers
      themselves — every exhibit carrying its document references, dates and page citations.</span></div></div>
