  <h2>The questions people actually ask</h2>
  <div class="caps" style="margin-top:30px;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(330px,1fr))"><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">Is the personal plan a cut-down version?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">No. It is the same build a company runs. Before you send reads the same specifications, the studios mark up the same drawings, all 43 calculators are there, and Before the pour watches the same pouring plan. What it does not carry is the machinery that only matters with a team: roles and permissions, the client portal, the cross-project view, automations. A single engineer has no use for any of those.</span></div><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">What if I work on more than one project?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">The personal plan holds one project at a time. You can change which project that is, but not run two at once &middot; that is the line between the personal plan and the company licence. If you routinely carry three jobs, ask about the company licence: it is not priced per person, so for one busy consultant it is often the sensible answer.</span></div><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">How do I pay, and can I stop?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">Today: you ask, a person answers, and you receive an invoice. There is no card form on this site yet &middot; when there is, it will be said here plainly. You stop by not renewing; there is no notice period and nothing to cancel by phone. Your documents were always on your own machine, so stopping costs you no data.</span></div><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">What happens to my work when the licence ends?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">Nothing is deleted, disabled or held. The app keeps running with an <b>Unlicensed</b> badge and every document stays exactly where it was, on your own disk. A tool used on a live site must never brick itself mid-project &middot; that is a design rule here, not a courtesy.</span></div><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">Does the price include the AI features?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">The licence covers the software. AI tasks &middot; the assistant, document reviews, the full outbound check &middot; run on <b>your own AI key</b>, which you paste into the app once. That is deliberate: you see the real cost and control it, and we never mark it up. Most of the product &middot; search, registers, claims, studios, calculators, the instant outbound pass &middot; needs no AI account at all.</span></div><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">Where does my data live?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">On the machine you run it on. There is no cloud tenancy in the middle, no copy of your specifications on our servers, and nothing to leak from a place you do not control. The <a href="security.html" style="color:var(--teal)">security page</a> sets out the whole picture.</span></div><div class="cap" style="padding:17px 19px"><b style="font-size:13.5px">Can I try it before paying anything?</b><span style="font-size:12.5px;margin-top:6px">Yes &middot; 15 days, the full product, on a realistic demo project that cannot touch anyone&rsquo;s live data. Most people start there. <a href="index.html#request" style="color:var(--teal)">Request a trial &rarr;</a></span></div></div>
