security = page_head(
    "Security — EngSpace · Engineering Workspace",
    "Where the data lives, how access works, and what we will show you before you trust us with a project.")
security += f"""
<header class="phero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="crumb"><a href="index.html">Engineering Workspace</a> / Security</div>
  <div class="eyebrow">Security &amp; architecture</div>
  <h1>Your record, your hardware,<br>our discipline.</h1>
  <p class="sub" style="margin-top:16px">Engineering correspondence is confidential and contractual.
  This page says plainly how the workspace protects it — and, just as plainly, what is architecture
  and what is not yet an independent audit.</p>
</div></header>
<div class="wrap">

  <div class="block">
    <div class="eyebrow">The architecture</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">Where everything lives.</h2>
    <div class="caps" style="margin-top:24px">
      <div class="cap"><b>Data on your infrastructure</b><span>Documents, the search index and the database live on the machine your company operates. Indexing, OCR and search run locally; a thousand questions cost nothing and leave nothing behind in a vendor cloud.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>One encrypted door</b><span>Remote access passes through an encrypted tunnel with an edge allow-list; there is no anonymous surface. Sign-in is always required, and failed logins are rate-limited and alerted.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Two-factor for administrators</b><span>Admin sessions can install updates, so a password alone is not accepted: administrators must enrol an authenticator app before their first session.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Roles that fence, server-side</b><span>Owner, admin, engineer, viewer, client, management. A client sign-in reaches one read-only portal route and nothing else — enforced in the API, not hidden in the interface. What the company switches off in the portal is never even sent, and an address typed by hand reveals nothing a role may not have.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Walked, not assumed</b><span>The boundary is not a claim on a page: before each release the whole product is walked twice, once as an ordinary engineer and once as an administrator who is not the owner, watching every request the browser makes. Anything a role should not see and still receives is a defect, and is fixed before the release ships.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>The operator's own machine stays theirs</b><span>Mail, calendar and notes read from the computer the workspace runs on belong to the person who owns that workspace — not to the administrator role. A customer's IT administrator can run their whole company in it and never reach them, unless the owner deliberately widens that.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Per-user encrypted secrets</b><span>Mail sign-ins and AI keys are stored encrypted per person. No user, and no API, can read another's mailbox or key.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Signed updates only</b><span>Update packages must carry a valid signature from the seller's key to install. A tampered package refuses itself.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Licences without a data path</b><span>Installed copies validate a signed licence file locally and renew it with a small daily check-in that carries version, machine code and document <em>counts</em> — never content. If the licence service is unreachable the app keeps working through a generous grace window, and an expired licence turns the workspace read-only; nothing is ever disabled or deleted remotely. A site tool must never brick mid-project.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Backups, two layers</b><span>Scheduled local backups of the record, plus an encrypted nightly backup of the application record to storage the operator chooses. Restores are part of the design, not an afterthought.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>An audit trail that remembers</b><span>Logins, changes, approvals, substitutions, escalations and deletions — who did what, permanently on the record.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Trials cannot touch you</b><span>Trial and demo accounts are pinned to a demo project. Live projects do not exist for them.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <div class="eyebrow">Honesty clause</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">What this page is, and is not.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="font-size:14.5px">Everything above is how the system is built and
    operated today. It is not yet an independent certification, and we will not pretend
    otherwise. Before the first multi-company rollout, the roadmap includes an independent
    penetration test, and practices are run ISO 27001-aligned ahead of any formal programme.</p>
    <div class="caps" style="margin-top:22px">
      <div class="cap"><b>On request, under NDA</b><span>The architecture and data-location pack, the backup and restore runbook, the update-signing design, and a security review call with the person who built it.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>In every workspace</b><span>A security one-pager for your IT department, produced from the app itself in one press.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Found something?</b><span>Report it to <a href="mailto:{EMAIL}" style="color:var(--teal)">{EMAIL}</a>. Security reports are answered personally and fast, and we credit responsible disclosure.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ctaband reveal">
    <div style="flex:1;min-width:260px"><h3>Bring your IT department.</h3>
    <div style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink2);margin-top:4px">The trial is the fastest way to see the architecture from the inside.</div></div>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="index.html#request">Request a trial →</a>
  </div>
  <div class="pn"><a href="about.html">← About</a><a href="proven.html">Proven in delivery →</a></div>
</div>"""
write_page("security.html", security + FOOT, priority="0.8")


