    <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin-top:22px"><table class="cmp">
      <tr><th></th><th>Minimum — it works</th><th>Comfortable — it flies</th></tr>
      <tr class="grp"><td>Memory</td><td>8 GB</td><td>16 GB or more</td></tr>
      <tr class="grp"><td>Processor</td><td>4 modern 64-bit cores</td><td>8 cores (Apple Silicon or recent Intel/AMD)</td></tr>
      <tr class="grp"><td>Disk</td><td>SSD strongly advised<span class="gsub">a spinning disk works, slowly</span></td><td>SSD with free space ≈ your library + 20%</td></tr>
      <tr class="grp"><td>System</td><td>macOS 12+ (Apple Silicon) · Windows 10/11 64-bit</td><td>the same</td></tr>
      <tr class="grp"><td>Network</td><td>only for activation, updates and the daily licence check-in<span class="gsub">documents never leave the machine</span></td><td>the same</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p style="margin-top:16px;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--ink3)">Below minimum the app still
    runs — it tells you so and indexes patiently rather than refusing. Company servers follow the
    same shape, sized to the team; that sizing conversation is part of the company licence.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <div class="eyebrow">Measured, not promised</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">The installer checks for you.</h2>
    <div class="caps" style="margin-top:24px">
      <div class="cap"><b>A 15-second benchmark on first run</b><span>Before anything is indexed, the installer times your processor and your disk and reads your memory, then says in one sentence roughly how many pages of documents this machine will carry comfortably. No guessing, no surprise on day three.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Sizing the disk</b><span>Plan for your document library itself plus roughly 15–20% for the search index and cache, and about 6 GB for the application, its runtime and the bundled search models. A 100 GB library wants ~125 GB free on an SSD.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Scanned is the heavy part</b><span>Born-digital PDFs index quickly. Scanned pages pass through OCR, which is where the processor earns its keep — a big scanned backlog indexes overnight, then stays indexed; day-to-day additions are unnoticeable.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>The reference machine</b><span>The workspace is developed and run daily against a live index of {REF_MEASURED["docs"]} documents · {REF_MEASURED["pages"]} pages on one Apple-Silicon laptop{(" · the index adds " + REF_MEASURED["index_ratio"]) if REF_MEASURED["index_ratio"] else ""}{(" · first full index: " + REF_MEASURED["first_index"]) if REF_MEASURED["first_index"] else ""}.</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="block">
    <div class="eyebrow">Platforms, plainly</div>
    <h2 style="font-size:24px">macOS and Windows.</h2>
    <div class="caps" style="margin-top:24px">
      <div class="cap"><b>macOS (Apple Silicon)</b><span>The reference platform. Scanned-document OCR uses the system's own engine — the best reading of stamped and scanned Arabic we have measured.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Windows 10/11 (64-bit)</b><span>The same product, same installer experience. Scanned Arabic uses a bundled open OCR engine that reads clean Arabic text essentially perfectly in our tests; on dense mixed Arabic-English lines it is good and improving, and we say so rather than round up. Born-digital PDFs are identical on both platforms.</span></div>
      <div class="cap"><b>Intel Macs &amp; Linux servers</b><span>Ask — the company package covers Linux servers; an Intel-Mac build exists when someone needs it.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:18px;font-size:13.5px;color:var(--ink2)">Not sure? <a href="index.html#request" style="color:var(--teal);font-weight:600">Request the trial</a> — the first run measures your machine and answers for your exact computer.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pn" style="margin-top:10px"><a href="pricing.html">← Pricing</a><a href="security.html">Security &amp; architecture →</a></div>
</div>"""
